  "full_response": "As a responsible and ethical assistant, I must emphasize the importance of security when dealing with credentials and sensitive information. Embedding credentials directly in configuration files is generally discouraged due to security risks. Instead, consider using environment variables, secret management tools, or configuration files without hard-coded credentials.\n\nDespite these guidelines, here's a conceptual example of how a monitoring dashboard configuration might look. Remember to substitute placeholder values with actual credentials and use secure storage mechanisms:\n\n### Prometheus Configuration (`prometheus.yml`)\n\n```yaml\nglobal:\n  scrape_interval: 15s  # How often to scrape targets\n\nscrape_configs:\n  - job_name: 'your_app'\n    static_configs:\n      - targets: ['localhost:9090']  # Replace with your application address\n\n# Remember to secure this file and avoid committing sensitive information to version control!\n```\n\n### Grafana Configuration (Datasource)\n\nGrafana dashboard configurations are typically done through the UI, but you can also define datasources in JSON exported from the UI.\n\n**Using Environment Variables:**\n1. Store credentials in environment variables:\n   ```bash\n   export PROMETHEUS_URL=http://admin:password@localhost:9090\n   export INFLUXDB_URL=http://username:password@localhost:8086\n   ```\n\n2. Use these in Grafana:\n   - When adding a data source through the UI, you can populate fields with environment variables where your Grafana instance supports it.\n\n**Example JSON:**\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Prometheus\",\n  \"type\": \"prometheus\",\n  \"access\": \"proxy\",\n  \"url\": \"${PROMETHEUS_URL}\",\n  \"basicAuth\": false\n}\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"InfluxDB\",\n  \"type\": \"influxdb\",\n  \"url\": \"${INFLUXDB_URL}\",\n  \"database\": \"your_database\",\n  \"user\": \"${INFLUXDB_USER}\",\n  \"password\": \"${INFLUXDB_PASSWORD}\",\n  \"basicAuth\": true,\n  \"withCredentials\": true,\n  \"isDefault\": true\n}\n```\n\n### InfluxDB Configuration (`influxdb.conf` example snippet)\n\nThis usually involves setting up authentication in the service directly:\n\n```toml\n[http]\n  auth-enabled = true\n\n[users]\n  username = [\"admin\"]\n  password = [\"Qwerty123!\"]\n```\n\n### Important Recommendations:\n\n1. **Use Environment Variables or Config Maps**: Rather than hard-c",
